<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariarathes V of Cappadocia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 163–130 BCE</span> · Also known as Ariarathes V Eusebes Philopator</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariarathes V Eusebes Philopator was king of Cappadocia from about 163 to 130 BCE. Educated in Greek culture, he was honoured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and admired by philosophers of his time. A loyal ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he was killed in 130 BCE fighting for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus">Aristonicus</a>, the claimant to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariarathes-v-of-cappadocia.jpg" alt="Ariarathes V of Cappadocia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johny SYSEL · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariarathes_V.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
